hich is  happening anyway and make it low carbon  and climate resilient and increasingly  we're seeing it needn't cost more in  fact it even costs less in many  instances renewable energy for example  now much cheaper  than coal in most parts of the world  we've had no new commitments no further  embroidering of china's position  how much of an issue is that  i think that's that's that for me is a  much bigger issue and is frankly  disappointing  um you know i was quite pleased with the  leadership that president xi showed back  in september of last year when he  announced  a net zero goal for china for 2060  but i really was hoping as many many  other observers were for a significant  tightening of china's target for 2030  and we've seen very little and i find  that a disappointing lack of leadership  from china  johnson biden david attenborough are all  evoking future generations they're the  ones that most at risk here what is the  the best the extensive youth movement  the youth activists that we're seeing  here  can expect from this conference directly  i think you know a lot of a lot of what  this conference needs  needs to deliver was done as we came in  before we got here we've got you know  commitments from most of the big  developed economies except australia i'm  afraid to say to pretty well halve their  emissions by 2030. that's a step forward  we're now seeing some of the big  emerging economies make ambitious  commitments that's great as well but  it's clear that we don't have remotely  enough yet  to put us on track so we need to bank  what we've got  uh i think we've got some quite exciting  sector announcements coming in the  course of the next week or so on you  know  reducing deforestation  committing to phase out internal  combustion engines  phasing out fossil fuel  financing  reducing methane emissions i think these  can build on those ndcs but i do think  at the end of this cop  there's going to have to be a  conversation  which many of the vu